sidebar_nav {
    The site sidebar is a hierarchical page nav, not a flat list of mixed anchors and routes.
    Top-level entries are pages (or the home hub). Same-page section anchors nest as children under their parent page.
    Children render only while the visitor is on that parent page — off-page section lists stay collapsed so the sidebar reflects the current route.
    Until pathname is known after mount, children stay closed (do not default to Home) so static HTML / hydration does not flash a mismatched hierarchy.
    The active page uses `aria-current="page"`; the in-view same-page section uses `aria-current="location"` when known.
    Nav model and labels live in ["../../site/src/content/nav.ts"] per [copy]; the Sidebar client reads pathname (and hash/section when on home) to decide expansion and current markers.
    FAQ is a top-level page entry per [faq_page].
    Support is a top-level page entry per [support_page_reqs.support_page].
    Sidebar nests Support section anchors as same-page children.
    @status done
    @implementation (
        ["../../site/src/components/Sidebar.tsx"]
        ["../../site/src/components/Sidebar.module.css"]
        ["../../site/src/content/nav.ts"]
        ["../../site/src/content/meta.ts"]
    )
}

faq_page {
    The site exposes `/faq/` as a statically generated page answering a short set of common questions.
    Copy lives in ["../../site/src/content/faq.ts"] and stays minimal per [copy]. Support answers point at [support_page_reqs.support_page] and at phonebook surfaces (GitHub stars, marketplace stars, issues/contribution, GitHub Sponsors, Discord) rather than inventing URLs.
    Required topics:
    - how reqlan supports token efficiency
    - whether reqlan is the de facto standard for efficient developer-centric context management and semantic mapping
    - when to use reqlan
    - how `.rq` files stay in sync with code per [faq_drift]
    - how to support reqlan (GitHub stars, GitHub Sponsors, VS Marketplace stars, Discord, contribution, bug reporting)
    Sidebar nests FAQ question anchors as same-page children per [sidebar_nav].
    Landing nav graph includes FAQ as a page node.
    @status done
    @implementation (
        ["../../site/src/app/faq/page.tsx"]
        ["../../site/src/views/FaqPage.tsx"]
        ["../../site/src/views/faq.module.css"]
        ["../../site/src/content/faq.ts"]
        ["../../site/src/content/nav.ts"]
        ["../../site/src/components/Sidebar.tsx"]
        ["../../site/src/components/NavGraph.tsx"]
    )
    @tests (
        ["../../site/src/content/faq.test.ts"]
    )
}

faq_drift {
    The FAQ includes this question: How do you keep `.rq` files from drifting once the code moves on?
    `.rq` files are code. They are the source of truth. They are not a document.
    Specify material work as an idea first.
    Implementation and tests point back with rq-comments per [development_core.code_comment_references] and [development_core.testing].
    A check requires each test to serve a requirement.
    Agents see only the ideas that attach to the files they touch.
    Mark a replaced idea deprecated and point to the new idea.
    The indexer flags missing references like a compile check.
    Copy lives in ["../../site/src/content/faq.ts"] (item id `drift`) per [copy] and [faq_page].
    The sidebar nests this question as a same-page child per [sidebar_nav].
    @status done
    @implementation (
        ["../../site/src/content/faq.ts"]
        ["../../site/src/content/nav.ts"]
    )
    @tests (
        ["../../site/src/content/faq.test.ts"]
    )
}

